// `com.microsoft::GroupQueryAttention` — DECODE ONLY, with the KV/past dimension split across the
// invocations of one workgroup.
//
// # What this file is, and what it deliberately is not
//
// `gqa_f16.comp` is the general kernel: one invocation per `(batch, query_head, query_seq_pos)`,
// no barriers, no shared memory, a plain serial online softmax over the whole cache.  It is frozen
// by this change — not one byte of it moves — and it remains the kernel for **every** shape except
// the one this file names.  This file is a second, narrower module, and the narrowing is the
// entire safety argument: at `seq_len == 1` three of `gqa_f16`'s branches are dead by arithmetic,
// not by assumption, and what is left is short enough to parallelise without inheriting the
// prefill correctness surface.
//
// The dead branches, and why they are dead rather than omitted:
//
//   * `s_local` is `gid % S` with `S == 1`, so `s_local == 0` identically.  `tok_pos` is
//     `past_len + s_local`, so `tok_pos == past_len`.
//   * `gqa_f16`'s attention loop runs `t` over `[0, tok_pos]` and splits it three ways:
//     `t < past_len` (the cache), `t == tok_pos` (this token's own key) and
//     `past_len <= t < tok_pos` (a key belonging to a *sibling query position of the same
//     dispatch*, recomputed from `packed_qkv` because reading it back out of `present` is a
//     data race).  With `tok_pos == past_len` that third interval is EMPTY.  The race, and the
//     recompute that repairs it, cannot arise here — which is why this kernel may use barriers
//     at all without re-opening the question `gqa_f16` settled by removing the read.
//   * `copy_leader`'s `s_local == 0` conjunct is a tautology, so it reduces to
//     `present_len != past_stride && kv_write_leader`.
//
// Nothing here is a re-derivation of `gqa_f16`'s arithmetic with a wider workgroup: a wider
// workgroup on `gqa_f16` is `local_size_x_id = 0`, it already exists, it is `gqa_local_size`'s
// job, and MEASURED it makes decode SLOWER (see `ops::attention::gqa_local_size`'s table: 32
// invocations at `local = 32` cost 209.19 ms against 135.56 ms at `local = 1`).  Packing more
// invocations into a workgroup does not help when there are only `B * Nq = 32` of them; what
// helps is giving each of those 32 units of work MORE lanes, and the only axis with enough
// extent to split is the KV/past dimension.  That axis is not partitionable without a combine,
// and the combine is what this file adds.
//
// # The mapping
//
//   one workgroup  <->  one `(batch b, query head h)` pair.  `gl_NumWorkGroups.x == B * Nq`.
//   W invocations  <->  a strided partition of `t` over `[0, tok_pos]`:  `t = lane, lane + W,
//                       lane + 2W, ...`.  `W` is specialisation constant 0.
//
// Strided (`t = lane; t <= tok_pos; t += W`) rather than blocked (`t = lane * chunk .. `) for
// two reasons that are both about portability rather than taste:
//
//   1. **Load balance is exact for every past length.**  Lane occupancy differs by at most one
//      iteration whatever `tok_pos` is, so there is no shape at which one lane runs the whole
//      cache while the rest wait at the barrier.  A blocked split has to round, and the rounding
//      is worst exactly where the kernel matters (short caches, where the tail lane owns the
//      remainder).
//   2. **Adjacent lanes read adjacent KV rows.**  Whatever the hardware's SIMD width is — and
//      this kernel is forbidden from knowing it — consecutive `lane` values touch consecutive
//      `t`, so the per-`d` loads of `past_key[.., t, d]` are contiguous across lanes.  A blocked
//      split puts them a whole chunk apart.
//
// # The combine, and why it is exact rather than approximate
//
// Each lane runs the SAME online softmax `gqa_f16` runs, restricted to its own subset of `t`:
// it holds `(m, s, a)` with `m = max_t score_t`, `s = sum_t exp(score_t - m)` and
// `a[d] = sum_t exp(score_t - m) * V[t][d]`.  Two such triples merge into the triple for the
// union of their `t` sets, exactly, with no error term:
//
//     m' = max(m1, m2)
//     s' = s1 * exp(m1 - m') + s2 * exp(m2 - m')
//     a'[d] = a1[d] * exp(m1 - m') + a2[d] * exp(m2 - m')
//
// This is the flash-decoding / FlashAttention-2 combine, evaluated at workgroup scope instead of
// across dispatches.  It is ALGEBRAICALLY exact and it is NOT bit-identical to the serial loop,
// because floating-point addition is not associative and the partition changes the summation
// order.  That is declared, not hidden: `docs/DESIGN.md` §8.14 states the tolerance, and
// `W == 1` is not this kernel at all — the host dispatches `gqa_f16` unchanged, so the
// production serial path stays bit-exact by *not running this file*.
//
// Empty lanes are not a special case.  A lane with no `t` (only possible when
// `W > tok_pos + 1`) carries `m = -1e30`, `s = 0`, `a = 0`; the merge multiplies its zeros by
// `exp(-1e30 - m') == 0` and adds nothing.  Lane 0 always owns `t = 0`, so the final `s` is
// never zero and the reciprocal below is never a division by zero.
//
// The `-1e30` sentinel is provably below every score this kernel can ever see, and that is a
// property of the input dtype rather than a hope about the data.  Every `score` is
// `dot(q, k) * scale` where `q` and `k` are decoded f16, so `|q[d]|, |k[d]| <= 65504`, the sum
// runs over `d < D_MAX == 128`, and `scale` arrives as an f32 push constant.  Hence
// `|score| <= 128 * 65504^2 * |scale| ~= 5.5e11 * |scale|`, which stays below `1e30` for any
// `|scale| < 1.8e18` — and `scale` is `1/sqrt(head_dim)`-shaped, i.e. at most 1.  An empty
// lane therefore cannot tie with, let alone outrank, a real score, so `max(m1, m2)` can never
// select the sentinel over a token that actually exists.
//
// # Non-finite policy: propagate, exactly as the serial kernel propagates
//
// This kernel does NOT sanitise, clamp or screen NaN/Inf.  It reproduces `gqa_f16`'s behaviour
// element for element, and the reason it is written down here is that a cooperative kernel has
// one failure mode a serial kernel does not: contaminating a row it was not asked to touch.
//
//   * A NaN score leaves `sc > max_s` false (every comparison against NaN is false), so `max_s`
//     survives, and then `e = exp(NaN - max_s)` is NaN, so `sum_e` and `acc` become NaN.  The
//     serial loop does exactly this.
//   * `sc = +INF` takes the rescale branch, drives `factor = exp(max_s - INF) = 0`, and lands on
//     `e = exp(INF - INF) = NaN`.  The serial loop does exactly this too.
//   * `sc = -INF` fails `sc > max_s` and contributes `e = 0`.  It is absorbed, in both kernels.
//   * In the merge, `max(m1, m2)` with a NaN operand is implementation-defined in GLSL — and it
//     does not matter, because the lane that produced the NaN already carries NaN in `s` and `a`,
//     so either choice of `m'` yields NaN in `s'` and `a'`.  Non-finiteness is absorbing under
//     this combine.
//
// The consequence is a per-row, all-or-nothing rule that the tests assert directly: a non-finite
// score anywhere in `(b, h)` makes all of `(b, h)`'s `attn_out` non-finite in BOTH kernels, and
// it can reach no other `(b, h)`, because `sh_m`/`sh_s`/`sh_a` are workgroup-local and one
// workgroup is exactly one `(b, h)`.  `present_key` and `present_value` are copies of the
// inputs and are not on the softmax path at all, so they are unaffected either way.
//
// # Portability: Vulkan 1.1 core, and nothing else
//
//   * **No subgroup anything.**  No `GL_KHR_shader_subgroup*`, no ballot, no shuffle, no
//     assumption that a workgroup is a lockstep unit.  The reduction is `shared` memory plus
//     `barrier()`, which is core since Vulkan 1.0.
//   * **Shared memory: 8,320 bytes**, fixed, independent of `W` and of `head_dim`
//     (`16*4 + 16*4 + 16*128*4`).  Vulkan 1.1 requires `maxComputeSharedMemorySize >= 16384`, so
//     this fits inside the guaranteed floor with 8,064 bytes — 49.2% — to spare on EVERY
//     conformant implementation, lavapipe included.  The constant array bounds are what make that
//     a property of the specification rather than of the device: sizing the arrays from `W` would
//     make the reported usage depend on a host decision, and a host decision cannot be checked by
//     reading this file.
//   * **`W <= 16` for the same reason**, and it is a consequence rather than a taste: at
//     `W = 32` the accumulator alone is `32*128*4 = 16384` bytes, which is the whole guaranteed
//     floor with nothing left for `sh_m`/`sh_s`.  `ops::attention::GQA_DECODE_MAX_KV_LANES`
//     states the same number on the host and its unit test cites this arithmetic.
//     `maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations` is required to be at least 128, so 16 is inside that
//     floor by a factor of 8 as well.
//   * **Every `barrier()` is reached by every invocation of the workgroup.**  The only early
//     return is `b >= batch_size`, which depends on `gl_WorkGroupID.x` alone — the same value for
//     all W lanes — so it is workgroup-uniform and cannot leave a lane behind at a barrier.  The
//     reduction's trip count is `ceil(log2(W))` where `W` is a specialisation constant, so it too
//     is uniform, and the `barrier()` sits OUTSIDE the `if` in the loop body, never inside it.
//   * **`head_dim <= 128`**, checked on the host (`GQA_DECODE_MAX_HEAD_DIM`), which is the bound
//     `gqa_f16`'s own `float q[128]` already assumed.  A wider head falls back to `gqa_f16`.
//
// # Push constants and bindings are `gqa_f16`'s, unchanged
//
// Byte-for-byte the same 36-byte block and the same 9 bindings in the same order, so
// `translate_gqa` builds one push vector and one binding list for both kernels and there is no
// second layout to drift.  `vk/session.rs`'s KV-convention witness reads offsets 24 and 28 of
// whichever of the two stems ran.
//
//   offset  0: batch_size   u32
//   offset  4: seq_len      u32   (always 1 here — the host guarantees it and `main` re-checks)
//   offset  8: num_heads    u32
//   offset 12: kv_num_heads u32
//   offset 16: head_dim     u32
//   offset 20: rotary_dim   u32
//   offset 24: present_len  u32
//   offset 28: past_stride  u32
//   offset 32: scale        f32
//
//   binding 0 packed_qkv, 1 past_key, 2 past_value, 3 seqlens_k, 4 cos_cache, 5 sin_cache,
//   binding 6 attn_output, 7 present_key, 8 present_value

#version 450
#extension GL_EXT_shader_16bit_storage : require

// Specialisation id 0 — `W`, the number of KV lanes in a workgroup.  Chosen by
// `ops::attention::gqa_decode_kv_lanes`, always a power of two in `[2, 16]` when this module is
// dispatched at all.  The default of 1 is not a production value: it exists so that a pipeline
// built without the constant degenerates to a correct single-lane run rather than to a zero-lane
// one, and the host never asks for it (`W == 1` dispatches `gqa_f16` instead).
layout(local_size_x_id = 0, local_size_x = 1, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;

// The compile-time ceilings the shared allocation is sized from.  See the portability note above:
// these are constants, not specialisation constants, precisely so that the 8,320-byte figure is
// readable here and cannot be moved by a host decision.
const uint W_MAX = 16u;
const uint D_MAX = 128u;

layout(push_constant) uniform PC {
    uint  batch_size;
    uint  seq_len;
    uint  num_heads;
    uint  kv_num_heads;
    uint  head_dim;
    uint  rotary_dim;
    uint  present_len;
    uint  past_stride;
    float scale;
} pc;

layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) readonly buffer BufQKV  { uint d[]; } qkv;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, std430) readonly buffer BufPK   { uint d[]; } past_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2, std430) readonly buffer BufPV   { uint d[]; } past_v;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3, std430) readonly buffer BufSeq  { int  d[]; } seqlens;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4, std430) readonly buffer BufCos  { uint d[]; } cos_buf;
layout(set = 0, binding = 5, std430) readonly buffer BufSin  { uint d[]; } sin_buf;
// Not `writeonly`: the half-word writes are read-modify-write atomics, exactly as in `gqa_f16`.
layout(set = 0, binding = 6, std430) buffer BufOut   { uint d[]; } attn_out;
layout(set = 0, binding = 7, std430) buffer BufPresK { uint d[]; } pres_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 8, std430) buffer BufPresV { uint d[]; } pres_v;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The workgroup's combine state.  8,320 bytes, see the header.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
shared float sh_m[W_MAX];
shared float sh_s[W_MAX];
shared float sh_a[W_MAX * D_MAX];

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scalar f16 helpers — identical to `gqa_f16`'s, per buffer because GLSL cannot parameterise a
// buffer block.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
float f16_qkv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(qkv.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pk(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_k.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_v.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_cos(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(cos_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_sin(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(sin_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}

// The atomics protect the *neighbouring half-word*, which two different writers can share when
// `head_dim` is odd.  Same rule, same reason, same code as `gqa_f16`.
void wr_out(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presk(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presv(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}

void main() {
    uint W    = gl_WorkGroupSize.x;   // the resolved specialisation constant
    uint lane = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    uint wg   = gl_WorkGroupID.x;

    uint Nq  = pc.num_heads;
    uint Nkv = pc.kv_num_heads;
    uint D   = pc.head_dim;
    uint rh  = pc.rotary_dim >> 1u;

    // One workgroup per (b, h).  `wg` is the only input to this decode, so `b` is
    // WORKGROUP-UNIFORM and the guard below cannot strand a lane at a barrier.
    uint b = wg / Nq;
    uint h = wg % Nq;
    // FAIL CLOSED.  The host dispatches exactly `B * Nq` workgroups, so this never fires in
    // production; it is here for the same reason `gqa_f16`'s is, and because a guard that only
    // exists when it is needed is a guard nobody checks.
    if (b >= pc.batch_size) return;
    // The host promises `seq_len == 1` and refuses to bind this module otherwise
    // (`gqa_decode_kv_lanes` returns 1 for every other `seq_len`, and 1 dispatches `gqa_f16`).
    // Re-checked here because the promise is on the other side of an ABI: a push constant that
    // says otherwise means the host and this file disagree, and the safe reading of a
    // disagreement is to write nothing.  Uniform for the whole dispatch, so barrier-safe.
    if (pc.seq_len != 1u) return;
    if (D > D_MAX || W > W_MAX) return;

    uint kv_h       = h / (Nq / Nkv);
    int  seqlens_b  = seqlens.d[int(b)];
    // `seqlens_k[b]` is `total_sequence_length - 1`, and `total = past + seq_len`, so the past
    // extent is `seqlens_k[b] + 1 - seq_len`.  With `seq_len == 1` that is `seqlens_k[b]` — the
    // one place the decode-only assumption `gqa_f16` had to unlearn is actually true.  It is
    // still written out in full so the two files read the same.
    uint past_len   = uint(max(seqlens_b + 1 - int(pc.seq_len), 0));
    uint tok_pos    = past_len;              // s_local == 0
    uint total_len  = tok_pos + 1u;          // causal: [0 .. tok_pos]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1./2. Q for this (b, h), rotated.  Every lane computes it: it is `O(D)` against the
    //       loop's `O(total_len/W * D)`, it is the same arithmetic on the same read-only bytes
    //       in every lane, and sharing it would cost a shared array and one more barrier.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint qkv_row = b * (Nq + 2u * Nkv) * D;   // (b * S + 0) * (...) * D, with S == 1
    uint q_base  = qkv_row + h * D;
    uint k_base  = qkv_row + Nq * D + kv_h * D;
    uint v_base  = qkv_row + (Nq + Nkv) * D + kv_h * D;

    float q[D_MAX];
    float acc[D_MAX];
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
        q[d]   = f16_qkv(q_base + d);
        acc[d] = 0.0;
    }
    {
        uint cos_row = tok_pos * rh;
        for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
            float c  = f16_cos(cos_row + ri);
            float sn = f16_sin(cos_row + ri);
            float qx = q[ri];
            float qy = q[ri + rh];
            q[ri]      = qx * c - qy * sn;
            q[ri + rh] = qy * c + qx * sn;
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. present_key/value at `tok_pos`, written by ONE lane of the group leader.
    //
    //    `gqa_f16` writes this from the `h % group_size == 0` leader, because `present` is
    //    indexed by `kv_h` and all G query heads of a KV group name the same words with the same
    //    values.  That deduplication is preserved.  What is new is that a workgroup now has W
    //    lanes and they would all be leaders, so the write is additionally pinned to `lane == 0`
    //    — the same one-writer-per-word discipline, one level down.
    //
    //    The rotated K is streamed rather than materialised: `gqa_f16` keeps `k_new[128]` because
    //    it needs it again for the self-key dot product, and this kernel does not (the lane that
    //    owns `t == tok_pos` recomputes it from `packed_qkv`, which is read-only input, exactly
    //    as `gqa_f16`'s sibling-key branch does).  Two 128-float private arrays saved per lane,
    //    times W lanes.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint group_size      = Nq / Nkv;
    bool kv_write_leader = (h % group_size) == 0u;
    if (kv_write_leader && lane == 0u && tok_pos < pc.present_len) {
        uint pb = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D + tok_pos * D;
        for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
            float c  = f16_cos(tok_pos * rh + ri);
            float sn = f16_sin(tok_pos * rh + ri);
            float kx = f16_qkv(k_base + ri);
            float ky = f16_qkv(k_base + ri + rh);
            wr_presk(pb + ri,      kx * c - ky * sn);
            wr_presk(pb + ri + rh, ky * c + kx * sn);
        }
        for (uint d = 2u * rh; d < D; d++) {
            wr_presk(pb + d, f16_qkv(k_base + d));
        }
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
            wr_presv(pb + d, f16_qkv(v_base + d));
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3a. The growing-cache past copy, fused into the loop below exactly as `gqa_f16` fuses it.
    //     `s_local == 0` is a tautology here, so the predicate loses that conjunct and nothing
    //     else.  Each past `t` is loaded by exactly one lane, so each is copied exactly once and
    //     the W lanes share the work rather than repeating it.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    bool copy_leader = pc.present_len != pc.past_stride && kv_write_leader;
    uint copy_dst    = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. This lane's slice of the online softmax.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    float max_s = -1.0e30;
    float sum_e = 0.0;

    for (uint t = lane; t < total_len; t += W) {
        float dot = 0.0;
        if (t < past_len) {
            uint kv_base = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D + t * D;
            if (copy_leader) {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                    float kv = f16_pk(kv_base + d);
                    dot += q[d] * kv;
                    wr_presk(copy_dst + t * D + d, kv);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * f16_pk(kv_base + d);
            }
        } else {
            // `t == tok_pos`: this token's own key.  Recomputed from `packed_qkv` — a read-only
            // dispatch input — rather than read back from `present_key`, which lane 0 of this
            // very workgroup may still be writing.  There is no barrier between step 3 and here
            // and there must not be one: adding it would make the write ordering the correctness
            // argument, and `gqa_f16` already established that reading `present` back is the
            // wrong shape of fix even when the writer is a workgroup sibling.
            for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
                float c  = f16_cos(tok_pos * rh + ri);
                float sn = f16_sin(tok_pos * rh + ri);
                float kx = f16_qkv(k_base + ri);
                float ky = f16_qkv(k_base + ri + rh);
                dot += q[ri] * (kx * c - ky * sn) + q[ri + rh] * (ky * c + kx * sn);
            }
            for (uint d = 2u * rh; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * f16_qkv(k_base + d);
        }
        float sc = dot * pc.scale;

        if (sc > max_s) {
            float factor = exp(max_s - sc);
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] *= factor;
            sum_e *= factor;
            max_s = sc;
        }
        float e = exp(sc - max_s);
        sum_e += e;

        if (t < past_len) {
            uint kv_base = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D + t * D;
            if (copy_leader) {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                    float vv = f16_pv(kv_base + d);
                    acc[d] += e * vv;
                    wr_presv(copy_dst + t * D + d, vv);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * f16_pv(kv_base + d);
            }
        } else {
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * f16_qkv(v_base + d);
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5. Combine the W partial triples.
    //
    //    Publish, then a tree reduction with `ceil(log2(W))` rounds.  The trip count is a
    //    function of the specialisation constant alone, so it is identical in every lane; the
    //    `barrier()` is the last statement of the loop BODY, not of the `if`, so every lane
    //    executes every barrier the same number of times.  That is the whole uniformity
    //    obligation and it is discharged structurally rather than by inspection.
    //
    //    The stride form `(lane & (2*stride - 1)) == 0` is correct for any `W`, power of two or
    //    not, and `partner < W` retires the odd tail.  The host only ever asks for powers of two,
    //    and this does not rely on that.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    sh_m[lane] = max_s;
    sh_s[lane] = sum_e;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) sh_a[lane * D_MAX + d] = acc[d];
    memoryBarrierShared();
    barrier();

    for (uint stride = 1u; stride < W; stride <<= 1u) {
        uint partner = lane + stride;
        if ((lane & ((stride << 1u) - 1u)) == 0u && partner < W) {
            float m1 = sh_m[lane];
            float m2 = sh_m[partner];
            float mm = max(m1, m2);
            float w1 = exp(m1 - mm);
            float w2 = exp(m2 - mm);
            sh_m[lane] = mm;
            sh_s[lane] = sh_s[lane] * w1 + sh_s[partner] * w2;
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                sh_a[lane * D_MAX + d] =
                    sh_a[lane * D_MAX + d] * w1 + sh_a[partner * D_MAX + d] * w2;
            }
        }
        memoryBarrierShared();
        barrier();
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6. attn_output[b, 0, h*D .. (h+1)*D - 1].  Lane 0 owns the whole row, so the atomics in
    //    `wr_out` never contend inside a workgroup and contend across workgroups only when `D`
    //    is odd — the same condition, and the same reason, as in `gqa_f16`.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (lane == 0u) {
        float inv_s = 1.0 / sh_s[0];
        uint  ob    = b * Nq * D + h * D;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) wr_out(ob + d, sh_a[d] * inv_s);
    }
}
